-- title:
--   Trivial Weil pairing for vanishing glued specialisations
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\kappa$ denotes the residue field of the valuation subring `Pl` of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$.
--
--   **Arithmetic base.** Fixed are a valuation subring `Pl` of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, a prime $p$, and a natural number $M$ with $5 \le M$ (`hM`) and $p \nmid M$ (`hpM`); a field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb Q$ of type $\{p\}$, together with $\zeta \in L$ a primitive $p$-th root of unity (`hζ`); an intermediate field $K$ of $L \subseteq L((q))$ which by `hK` equals [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M*p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), that is the $L$-subfield of the Laurent series field generated by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb Q$; a discrete valuation ring $A$ which is a domain with fraction field $L$, with $p$ in its maximal ideal (`hAp`) and with $\zeta$ in the image of $A \to L$ (`hζA`), and $K$ an $A$-algebra compatibly with $A \to L \to K$; and an element $j \in K$, assumed nonzero, whose Laurent expansion is the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of the $j$-function (`hj`). Write $x_A :=$ [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) for the structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ of the two-chart model obtained by glueing the spectra of the integral closures of $A[j]$ and $A[j^{-1}]$ in $K$; it is assumed proper. The residue field $\kappa$ of `Pl` is assumed algebraically closed, of characteristic $p$, and an $A$-algebra.
--
--   **Special fibre and its two components.** Given are two proper, smooth of relative dimension one, geometrically integral curves $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec}\kappa$ and $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec}\kappa$ and closed immersions $i_1, i_2$ of them, over $\kappa$, into the base change of $x_A$ to $\kappa$; `hcover` states that every point of that base change lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` that the fibre product of $i_1$ and $i_2$ is reduced; and `hn`, `hn0` that its cardinality is $n$ with $0 < n$.
--
--   **Sections.** A section $\varepsilon$ of $x_A$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, sections $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2$ of $c_1, c_2$, and `hε₁`: $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $\kappa$.
--
--   **Relative Picard data.** $D$ is a `RelativePic0Designation` for $x_A$, i.e. a scheme $D.P$ with a morphism $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a zero section; `hrep` asserts that $D$ represents, with Poincaré bundle and universal property, the functor of rigidified line bundles on $x_A$ which on each geometric fibre are algebraically equivalent to zero (the condition `algEquivZeroCut`), and `hsm`, `hsep` that $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ is smooth and separated. The same representability over $\kappa$ for the base changed designation is `hreps`, and `hPk` identifies its Poincaré bundle with the base change of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep`; $D_1, D_2$ with `hrep₁`, `hrep₂` are the corresponding designations for $c_1, c_2$ over $\kappa$. A morphism $\nu_2$ from the $\kappa$-base change of $D$ to $D_2$ over $\kappa$ is given, with `hν₂` expressing that for any $\kappa$-scheme point $a$ of the former, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep₂` along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification along the section $\varepsilon_2$ of the pullback, along the curve change induced by $i_2$, of the bundle pulled back along $a$.
--
--   **Embeddings into $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and fibrewise hypotheses.** Algebra structures $A \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$ and $L \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$ forming a tower; `hsmL`, `hgiL`: the base change of $x_A$ to $L$ is smooth of relative dimension one and geometrically integral; `hprL`, `hgcL`: the second projection of the pullback of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ along $\operatorname{Spec} L \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ is proper and geometrically connected.
--
--   **Geometric model and its $q$-expansion pinning.** $M\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M*p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) (an integral, proper, smooth relative dimension one scheme with an identification of its function field, and a bijection between closed points and places), $e\eta$ an isomorphism from $M\eta.C$ to the geometric fibre of $x_A$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, compatible with the structure morphisms (`heη`). The instance `Mη_chart_nonempty` records that the preimage of the finite chart is nonempty, and `hMηpin` states that for every element $a$ of the finite chart algebra `TwoChart.chartAlgFin A K j` the corresponding function field element of $M\eta$, obtained via $e\eta$ and the germ at the generic point, has Laurent expansion the coefficientwise image under $L \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the expansion of $a$. The hypothesis `hgal` is Galois equivariance of this identification: for $g \in \operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ fixing $L$ pointwise and two $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points $x, x'$ of $M\eta.C$ whose chart components satisfy $x' = g$ followed by $x$, the place attached to $x'$ is the image of the place attached to $x$ under the coefficientwise semilinear automorphism [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54) of $g$.
--
--   **Hecke inputs.** `hin` is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M*p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L58) and `hcomm` is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondCommuteBar (M*p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L54) (commutation of the Hecke and diamond generators on the geometric function field).
--
--   **Galois action on $A$.** A multiplicative semiring action of $\operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb Q)$ on $A$ with `hΓA`: $A \to L$ is equivariant.
--
--   **Special fibre group data.** $G$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9): abelian groups $G.J0s$, $G.JI$, $G.JE$, a subgroup $G.\mathrm{torus}$ of $G.J0s$, and a surjection $G.\mathrm{proj} : G.J0s \to G.JI \times G.JE$ with kernel $G.\mathrm{torus}$. Bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` identify these three groups with the $\kappa$-points of the $\kappa$-base change of $D$, of $D_1$ and of $D_2$; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` state that addition corresponds to tensor product of the pulled-back Poincaré bundles; `hproj` states that the two components of $G.\mathrm{proj}$ correspond, under these bijections, to postcomposition with the pullback morphism attached to $i_1$ and with $\nu_2$; `hPTS` states that `pts` is additive for the relative group law of `hreps` and sends $0$ to its identity.
--
--   **Global points and operators.** `gpts` is a bijection from $J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb Q}) =$ [`ModularCurve.JOne (M*p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186) to the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points of $D$; $\varphi$ assigns to each element of [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) (the polynomial ring on the Hecke and diamond generators) an endomorphism of $D$ over $A$, and $\tau$ assigns to each $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb Q)$ a morphism of $D$ over the $s$-twist of $\operatorname{Spec} A$. The hypotheses `hφmul` ($\varphi$ is additive for the relative group law), `hφpts` (`gpts` intertwines the Hecke module structure `heckeModuleOneBar` with $\varphi$), `hτ1`, `hτmul` (multiplicativity of $\tau$), `hτφ` ($\tau$ and $\varphi$ commute), `hgadd` (`gpts` is additive) and `hτpts` (`gpts` is Galois equivariant: for $\sigma'$ on $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ restricting to $s$ on $L$, the point of $\sigma' \cdot x$ is $\operatorname{Spec}\sigma'$ followed by the point of $x$ followed by $\tau(s^{-1})$) describe their interaction.
--
--   **Abel–Jacobi data.** `hDL` is the representability statement over $L$; `ajL` is a morphism from the $L$-base change of $x_A$ to the $L$-base change of $D$ with `hajLε` (it carries the section $\varepsilon$ to the zero section), `hPL` (base change compatibility of Poincaré bundles) and `hajL` (for every field $K'$ over $L$ and every point $x$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by `ajL` is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the section); `kL` is the comparison morphism from the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-fibre to the $L$-fibre, with `hkL₁`, `hkL₂` its compatibilities with the two projections; `ajbar` is defined by `hajbar` as $e\eta$ followed by `kL`, `ajL` and the first projection, and lies over the base by `hajbar_over`; $\bar\varepsilon$ is a $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-point of $M\eta.C$ over the cusp section (`hεbar`) which `ajbar` sends to the zero section (`hεbar_aj`); and `hpts_aj` states that for $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points $x$ and $s$ of $M\eta.C$, with $s$ the cusp section, there is a degree-zero divisor equal to $[\,\text{place of } x\,] - [\,\text{place of } s\,]$ whose class under `gpts` is $x$ followed by `ajbar`.
--
--   **Special fibre operators.** $O$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreOpsV3 G`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePOpsV3.html#L13) (Hecke operators on the three groups, diamond operators indexed by $(\mathbb Z/p)^\times$ and by integers, the Atkin–Lehner involution, an inertia action, and the stated commutation and triviality-on-the-torus relations). The hypothesis `hO` has four clauses: the $\kappa$-points of $O.\mathrm{hecke}\,\ell$ ($\ell$ prime), of $O.\mathrm{diamondP}\,b$ (for $d$ coprime to $Mp$ with $d \equiv 1 \bmod M$ and $d \equiv b \bmod p$), of $O.\mathrm{diamondN}\,d$ (for $d$ coprime to $Mp$ with $d \equiv 1 \bmod p$) and of $O.\mathrm{inertia}\,b$ (for $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb Q)$ with $s\zeta = \zeta^{b}$) are computed, after composition with the first projection, by postcomposition with $\varphi$ of the corresponding Hecke or diamond generator, respectively with $\tau(s)$.
--
--   **The place `Pl`.** `hPl`: $p$ lies in the nonunits of `Pl`; $\rho : A \to$ `Pl` with `hρ` (it induces $A \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$) and `hAlgκ` ($A \to \kappa$ is the residue map composed with $\rho$).
--
--   **Points over the inertia-fixed rings (`hF3`).** For every subgroup $I$ of $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ contained in the inertia subgroup of `Pl` (`hI`), fixing every $p$-th root of unity (`hIμ`) and of finite index in that inertia subgroup (`hIf`), setting $\mathcal O_I :=$ `Pl` $\cap\, \overline{\mathbb Q}^{\,I}$, there exist $\rho_I : A \to \mathcal O_I$ inducing $A \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$ such that, writing $\mathrm{dom}$ for the set of $x \in J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ whose point `gpts x` factors through an $\mathcal O_I$-point of $D$: (i) $I$ fixes $\mathrm{dom}$ pointwise; (ii) two $\mathcal O_I$-points with the same restriction to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ coincide; (iii) $0 \in \mathrm{dom}$ and $\mathrm{dom}$ is closed under subtraction; (iv) for every $n > 0$ with $p \nmid n$, an $n$-torsion $\mathcal O_I$-point with trivial reduction to $\kappa$ is trivial, and every $n$-torsion $\kappa$-point is the reduction of an $n$-torsion $\mathcal O_I$-point; (v) $\mathrm{dom}$ is stable under Frobenius elements at `Pl` normalising $I$.
--
--   **Néron model of the norm-free part (`h𝒜`).** Given $\mathcal A$ with a morphism $a$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and $\iota$ a morphism to $D$ over $a$, the hypothesis `h𝒜` asserts: $\iota$ is a closed immersion; $a$ is proper and smooth with connected geometric fibres over every algebraically closed field; the image of $\mathcal A$ is closed under the identity, multiplication and inversion of the relative group law of `hrep`; a point $x$ of $J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ lies in [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M*p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) if and only if `gpts x` factors through $\mathcal A$; and $\mathcal A$ is stable under each operator $\varphi(t)$.
--
--   **Vanishing of the norm operator (`hF10`).** For every commutative $\kappa$-algebra $T'$ and every $T'$-point $v$ of the $\kappa$-base change of $D$ whose first component factors through $\mathcal A$ over the base, composing that component with $\varphi$ of $\sum_{b \in (\mathbb Z/p)^\times} \langle d_b \rangle$, where $d_b$ is the natural number representing the Chinese remainder element $(b,1)$ modulo $M p$, yields the identity point.
--
--   **Igusa models and $q$-expansion reading.** $w$ is an `IntegralWeightOneForm` over $\kappa$ of level $M$ (a weight-one form on $\Gamma_1(M)$ with an integral $q$-expansion whose reduction in $\kappa((q))$ is nonzero); $\mathrm{Mdl}_1$ and $\mathrm{Mdl}_2$ are curve models over $\kappa$ of the Igusa function field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C κ M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35) (the $\kappa$-subfield of $\kappa((q))$ generated by the $q$-expansion function field of $X_1(M)$ and the inverse of the reduced expansion of $w$), with isomorphisms $e_1 : \mathrm{Mdl}_1.C \cong C_1$ and $e_2 : \mathrm{Mdl}_2.C \cong C_2$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`he₁`, `he₂`); `hne₁` records a nonemptiness of the relevant chart preimage, and `hgauss₁` pins the identification for $C_1$: for $a$ in the finite chart algebra and power series $x, y$ over $A$ with $y$ having nonzero reduction and $a \cdot y = x$ in $L((q))$, the function field element corresponding to $a$ has Laurent expansion the quotient of the reductions of $x$ and $y$. Further, `hεC₂` states that the reduction of the cusp section avoids the image of $i_2$, and `hεgal` that $\varepsilon$ is equivariant for the automorphisms of the two-chart model induced by $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb Q)$ acting coefficientwise on the chart algebra.
--
--   **Glued description of the special fibre Picard group (`hF4c`).** A finite set `nodesIg` of pairs of places of the Igusa function field is given, together with isomorphisms $\Psi : G.J0s \cong$ `GluedPic0 κ (igusaFunctionFieldX1C κ M w) nodesIg` (admissible pairs of degree-zero divisors vanishing at the marked places, together with a family of elements of $\kappa^\times$, modulo glued principal data) and $\theta_1, \theta_2$ from $G.JI$, $G.JE$ to the degree-zero Picard group of that field. The hypothesis `hF4c` asserts: a pair belongs to `nodesIg` exactly when it arises from a $\kappa$-point of the intersection of $i_1$ and $i_2$ through the place bijections of $\mathrm{Mdl}_1, \mathrm{Mdl}_2$; the cardinality of `nodesIg` is $n$; both projections are injective on `nodesIg`; `GluedPic0.toPic0Pair` composed with $\Psi$ equals $(\theta_1, \theta_2)$ composed with $G.\mathrm{proj}$; the image of $G.\mathrm{torus}$ under $\Psi$ is the range of `GluedPic0.nodeUnit`; and $\theta_1$, $\theta_2$ are computed by Abel–Jacobi classes $[x] - [\varepsilon_1]$, respectively $[x] - [\varepsilon_2]$, whenever the pullback of the corresponding Poincaré bundle is isomorphic to the line bundle of the divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the section. Finally `frobT` is a semilinear automorphism of the Igusa function field over $\kappa$ acting on Laurent coefficients by $p$-th powers (`hfrobT`).
--
--   **The abstract specialisation family.** For each $I$ as in `hF3` there are given an additive subgroup `dom𝒟 I …` of $J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ and an additive map `sp𝒟 I …` from it to `GluedPic0 κ (igusaFunctionFieldX1C κ M w) nodesIg`, subject to the interface `hIFm`: membership of $y$ in `dom𝒟 I …` is equivalent to `gpts y` factoring through an $\mathcal O_I$-point of $D$ (for the ring homomorphism chosen from `hF3`), and for such a point $z$ and every $\kappa$-point $u$ whose first component is the reduction of $z$, the value `sp𝒟 I … ⟨y, hy⟩` equals $\Psi(\mathrm{pts}^{-1} u)$. The hypothesis `hlev` has two clauses: for $J \le I$ (both admissible) one has $\mathcal O_I \subseteq \mathcal O_J$ and every $\mathcal O_I$-point $z$ representing `gpts y` restricts to an $\mathcal O_J$-point $z_J$ representing it with the same reduction to $\kappa$; and `dom𝒟 I …` is contained in `dom𝒟 J …` with the two specialisation maps agreeing there.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under all of the above, for every subgroup $I$ of $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ contained in the inertia subgroup of `Pl` over $\mathbb Q$, fixing every $p$-th root of unity in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, and with $I$ of finite index in that inertia subgroup; for every natural number $n$ (a newly bound variable, shadowing the earlier node count) with $0 < n$ and $p \nmid n$; for every Weil datum $d$ of level $n$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ for the field `laurentBaseChange (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) (x1FunctionField (M*p))`, that is divisors $d.D_1, d.D_2$ and nonzero functions $d.f_1, d.f_2$ with $\operatorname{ord}_v(d.f_1) = n\, d.D_1(v)$ and $\operatorname{ord}_v(d.f_2) = n\, d.D_2(v)$ at every place $v$, with $d.D_1$ and $d.D_2$ having disjoint supports and all places in their joint support rational; and for all degree-zero divisors $E_1, E_2$ whose underlying divisors equal $d.D_1$ and $d.D_2$ respectively: if the class $\mathrm{Pic}^0$-class of $E_1$ lies in `dom𝒟 I …` (witness $h_1$), the class of $E_2$ lies in `dom𝒟 I …`, and
--   $$\mathrm{toPic0Pair}(\mathtt{nodesIg})\bigl(\mathrm{sp}_{\mathcal D}(I)\langle [E_1], h_1\rangle\bigr) = 0$$
--   in the product of two copies of the degree-zero Picard group of the Igusa function field, then $d.\mathrm{pairing} = 1$, i.e. $\prod_v v(d.f_1)^{d.D_2(v)} = \prod_v v(d.f_2)^{d.D_1(v)}$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q}^\times$, the two products being taken in the sense of `Divisor.evalFun`.
--
--   This is the orthogonality clause in the construction of the pinned specialisation datum for the norm-free part of $J_1(Mp)$: a class whose specialisation into the glued Picard group of the special fibre lies in the toric part (equivalently, has vanishing image in the Picard groups of the two Igusa components) pairs trivially, via the Weil pairing computed from a level-$n$ Weil datum, with any class in the same domain. It is used by [`ModularCurve.exists_qExpSemistableSpecializationPinnedV3_family_normFreePart_and_diamond_of_dvd_of_not_sq_dvd_of_le_div`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_qExpSemistableSpecializationPinnedV3_family_normFreePart_and_diamond_of_dvd_of_not_sq_dvd_of_le_div), which assembles the specialisation family at $p$ entering the level-lowering step.
